The assertion that the middle component of a packed triple lies in Fun of the complements of the neighbourhoods of the outer two components is recursively decidable. The packing is (n, (j, m)), and the criterion used is a bounded existential quantification over c below n.

The bit test of the middle component at the outer component is recursively decidable by bitAt. The conjunction of equality of the untagged value with the target and the universal bit test over the decoded untagged list is recursively decidable by transporting the corresponding decider along the pairing of the witness with the remaining pair. Conjoining the bit test and closing under a bounded existential quantification along the left projection decides the predicate, by the bounded Fun criterion after simplifying the unpairing.
